ving warrior is\\n34-       awarded points calculated from a score formula (F).  By  default,  this\\n35-       is  (W*W-1)/S, where W is the total number of warriors participating, S\\n36-       is the number of of survivors, and \\\"/\\\" is integer division. Alternative\\n37-       score formulas can be specified with the = option (see below).\\n38-\\n39-       When two warriors battle, the results are reported as wins1, wins2, and\\n40-       ties, where wins1 and wins2 are the numbers of rounds that each warrior\\n41-       won,  and  ties  are the number of rounds in which both warriors lasted\\n42-       until time-out.  The score is then:\\n43-\\n44-               warrior 1:  points = wins1 * F + ties * F\\n45-               warrior 2:  points = wins2 * F + ties * F\\n46-\\n47-       F is a score formula containing the W and S parameters.  When more than\\n--\\n144:       -e     If this option is specified, the cdb debugger is entered immedi-\\n145-              ately after loading warriors into core.\\n146-\\n147-       -b     This options runs pMARS in brief output mode. -b suppresses war-\\n148-              rior listings after assembly.\\n149-\\n150-       -A     The  -A  option assembles the input warriors without running any\\n151-              fights.  Emits ICWS'94 draft compliant load files.\\n152-\\n153-       -k     With the -k option, pMARS uses the same  output  format  as  the\\n154-              KotH  program.  This  option  enables pMARS to work with scripts\\n155-              written for the KotH server.\\n156-\\n157-       -8     This options enforces strict compliance with the  ICWS'88  stan-\\n158-              dard  and  disables  all  ICWS'94 language extensions, which are\\n159-              flagged as syntax errors by the assembler. Since  ICWS'94  is  a\\n160-              superset  of  ICWS'88,  this  options  is  not  necessary to run\\n161-              ICWS'88 warriors.\\n162-\\n163-       -o     When this option is given, pMARS reports  scores  in  decreasing\\n164-              order  rather  than in the order warriors were named on the com-\\n165-              mand line. This is mostly useful for multi-warrior play.\\n166-\\n167-       -V     The assembler generates verbose output with this option. This is\\n168-              only useful for debugging.\\n169-\\n170-       -v     This  option sets the display mode for UNIX and DOS display ver-\\n171-              sions (see APPENDIX).\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 3445,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 862,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}"}}
